/target
.vscode
.claude
*.out

# Build artifacts emitted by the examples. Examples check in NO emitted output:
# multi-entry projects write `dist/`, single-entry ones write the bundle (and its
# stylesheet, and — for a browser leg — its build manifest) beside the source.
vilan/examples/**/dist/
vilan/examples/**/*.js
vilan/examples/**/*.mjs
vilan/examples/**/*.css
vilan/examples/**/*.chunks.json

# The benchmark bundle (`vilan run vilan/benchmarks` writes it beside the source).
vilan/benchmarks/src/main.mjs
vilan/docs/book/

# The union-suite log every integration writes beside the tree (three
# tracked-by-accident incidents in one day; the hygiene gate caught two).
suite.log

# The corpus filesystem program's scratch tree, left behind by a run that DIED.
# `vilan/test/file.vl` makes `file-corpus-<random>/` relative to the working
# directory and removes it at the end; the corpus differentials in
# `crates/vilan-core` spawn node with the crate directory inherited (N52's
# `corpus_harness::run_node_within` passes no `current_dir`), so a panic, a
# timeout kill or a Ctrl-C between the `create_dir_all` and the `remove_dir_all`
# strands one there. Ungitignored it shows up as an untracked directory in the
# next `git status` of whichever lane happens to look.
#
# An ignore line and NOT a harness `Drop` guard, deliberately: the harness never
# learns the random suffix (only the basename `data.txt` reaches stdout, by
# N54's design), so a guard could only glob `file-corpus-*` in the working
# directory — and that directory is shared by every concurrent run of the
# program, which is precisely N54's hazard. A cleanup that deletes a sibling
# process's live scratch tree is worse than a stray directory.
file-corpus-*/
